## `findFirst` Function

### Implementation

```javascript
/**
 * Returns the first element in an array that satisfies the predicate function.
 * @param {Array} arr - The array to search through
 * @param {Function} predicate - A function that returns true for the desired element
 * @returns {*} The first matching element, or null if none found
 */
function findFirst(arr, predicate) {
  if (!Array.isArray(arr)) {
    throw new TypeError(`Expected an array, but received: ${typeof arr}`);
  }

  if (typeof predicate !== "function") {
    throw new TypeError(`Expected a function, but received: ${typeof predicate}`);
  }

  for (let i = 0; i < arr.length; i++) {
    if (predicate(arr[i], i, arr)) {
      return arr[i];
    }
  }

  return null;
}
```

### Usage Examples

```javascript
// Find first even number
findFirst([1, 3, 4, 6, 7], (n) => n % 2 === 0);
// → 4

// Find first user over 18
const users = [
  { name: "Alice", age: 16 },
  { name: "Bob",   age: 22 },
  { name: "Carol", age: 30 },
];
findFirst(users, (user) => user.age >= 18);
// → { name: "Bob", age: 22 }

// No match found
findFirst([1, 3, 5], (n) => n % 2 === 0);
// → null

// Using index parameter in predicate
findFirst(["a", "b", "c"], (_, index) => index > 1);
// → "c"

// Works with falsy values in the array
findFirst([0, null, false, "", 42], (n) => typeof n === "number" && n > 0);
// → 42
```

### Key Design Decisions

| Decision | Reason |
|---|---|
| Returns `null` (not `undefined`) | More explicit "not found" signal; easier to check |
| Passes `(element, index, array)` to predicate | Mirrors native `Array.prototype.find` API |
| Input validation with `TypeError` | Fails fast with a clear message instead of silently misbehaving |
| Plain `for` loop | Short-circuits immediately on first match without extra overhead |

